      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Real Estate",
      "description": "Position Details - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Position Details Position Details Assistant Teaching Professor in Real Estate Position Overview School / Campus / College: College of Built Environments Organization: Runstad Department of Real Estate Title: Assistant Teaching Professor in Real Estate Position Details Position Description The Runstad Department of Real Estate in the College of Built Environments at the University of Washington invites applications for a teaching faculty position at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor, with an anticipated start date of September 2027. We seek applicants who demonstrate commitment to excellence in teaching and service in a diverse and inclusive academic community. The successful candidate will join our faculty on a full-time basis for a nine-month academic appointment beginning September 16, 2027. The base salary range for this position will be $10,000 - $12,000 per month, commensurate with experience and qualifications, or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. The position carries a 9-month service period, running from September 16 through June 15 each year, with potential summer salary contingent on programmatic, curricular, and administrative needs. The initial appointment is a three‑year term, with eligibility for renewal of three- to five-year terms based on performance. Assistant Teaching Professor positions are promotable, non-tenurable, and voting eligible. Assistant Teaching Professors at the University of Washington play a central role in fulfilling the institution’s educational mission. They design, deliver, and assess instruction across undergraduate and graduate programs, directly supporting student learning and academic success. The successful candidate will be expected to be on campus during the academic year to teach courses, mentor students, and engage in service activities, including service on departmental and college committees. Selected candidates will be required to teach 24 credit hours during each academic year and may be assigned a combination of large lecture courses and small seminar courses, spanning both undergraduate and graduate levels, as well as required and elective offerings across the curriculum. The Runstad Department of Real Estate within the College of Built Environments was established in 2017. The Department offers a Real Estate Major, a Real Estate Minor, a Master of Science in Real Estate, a Graduate Certificate in Real Estate, and a Graduate Certificate in Housing Studies. Together, these programs currently serve over 400 students. For more information about the Department, visit: https://re.be.uw.edu. Qualifications Applicants for this teaching faculty position must meet the following minimum qualifications: 1) Master’s degree (or foreign equivalent) in real estate, urban planning, construction management, geography, economics, public policy, business related disciplines, or another field with clear overlap in real estate theory or practice, with degree completion required no later than September 2027. 2) Evidence, documented in the application materials, of a strong record of outstanding teaching or professional practice expertise in real estate or a closely related discipline. Instructions To ensure full consideration of your application, please submit all materials no later than October 15, 2026. Priority will be given to those applications received by that date. Applications will only be accepted via Interfolio: apply.interfolio.com/188857 . The position will remain open until filled. Applicants should articulate in their application documents how their past experiences and/or future plans in teaching, mentoring, and/or service promote a sense of belonging and foster a welcoming and inclusive learning environment. Applications should include the following materials: 1) A cover letter detailing your interest in the position, relevant qualifications, and experiences (maximum length of two single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 2) A curriculum vitae or resume (maximum length of five single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 3) A statement of teaching philosophy and evidence of teaching effectiveness (maximum length of two single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 4) A complete list of existing departmental courses, ranked in order of teaching preference, and any new courses you would like to propose to create and teach. For each course, include a brief description of your qualifications to teach it (maximum length of three single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 5) Contact information for at least three referees who are prepared to provide letters of recommendation upon request (maximum length of one single‑spaced page, using 12‑point font) Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Equal Opportunity The University of Washington is committed to fostering an inclusive, respectful and welcoming community for all. As an equal opportunity employer, the University considers applicants for employment without regard to race, color, creed, religion, national origin, citizenship, sex, pregnancy, age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, disability, or veteran status consistent with UW Executive Order No. 81 . Benefits Information The University is committed to providing excellent benefits to our faculty. For a summary of available benefits, please see UWHR benefits information . Privacy Notice Your application materials may be used for employment consideration and shared in accordance with UW’s Privacy Policy. Disability Services The University of Washington is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education, and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact the Disability Services Office at 206-543-6450 or dso@uw.edu. Apply now The University of Washington is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. For help signing up, accessing your account, or submitting your application, please check out Interfolio's help and support section or get in touch via email at help@interfolio.com or phone at (877)997-8807. Title IX Notice The University of Washington does not discriminate on the basis of sex and prohibits sex discrimination and sex-based harassment in its education programs and activities, including in admission and employment. Refer to the UW policies page on the Title IX website to review (1) the University’s policies prohibiting sex- and gender-based violence, harassment, and discrimination and (2) the related procedures for addressing complaints. Inquiries about Title IX may be referred to the University’s Title IX Coordinator , the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights , or both. To report information or make a complaint about conduct that may constitute sex discrimination or sex-based harassment, submit an online Title IX report or email or call the Office of the Title IX Coordinator. Office of the Title IX Coordinator Valery Richardson, Title IX Coordinator 4311 11th Ave NE Seattle, WA 98105 206-221-7932 TitleIX@uw.edu ← Back to all positions University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
    },
    "https://ap.washington.edu/ahr/position-details/?job_id=186560": {
      "title": "Associate Teaching Professor in Real Estate and Director of Programs",
      "description": "Position Details - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Position Details Position Details Associate Teaching Professor in Real Estate and Director of Programs Position Overview School / Campus / College: College of Built Environments Organization: Runstad Department of Real Estate Title: Associate Teaching Professor in Real Estate and Director of Programs Position Details Position Description The Runstad Department of Real Estate in the College of Built Environments at the University of Washington invites applications for a teaching faculty position at the rank of Associate Teaching Professor and Director of Programs, with an anticipated start date of September 2027. We seek applicants who demonstrate commitment to excellence in teaching and service in a diverse and inclusive academic community. The successful candidate will join our faculty on a full-time basis for a nine-month academic appointment beginning September 16, 2027. The base salary range for this position will be $12,000 - $14,000 per month, commensurate with experience and qualifications, or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. The position carries a 9-month service period, running from September 16 through June 15 each year. The Director of Programs role receives the equivalent of one month of additional compensation. The initial appointment for the Associate Teaching Professor and Director of Programs is a three‑year term, with eligibility for renewal of three- to five-year terms based on performance. Associate Teaching Professor positions are promotable, non-tenurable, and voting eligible. Associate Teaching Professors at the University of Washington play a central role in fulfilling the institution’s educational mission. They design, deliver, and assess instruction across undergraduate and graduate programs, directly supporting student learning and academic success. The successful candidate will be expected to be on campus during the academic year to teach courses, mentor students, and engage in service activities, including service on departmental and college committees. Selected candidates will be required to teach 24 credit hours during each academic year and may be assigned a combination of large lecture courses and small seminar courses, spanning both undergraduate and graduate levels as well as required and elective offerings across the curriculum. While serving in the Director of Programs role, the position will receive a 3-credit course release each year. The Director of Programs provides leadership for the planning, implementation, and ongoing improvement of the department’s academic programs. Their role includes: overseeing and implementing curriculum and course updates; coordinating class offerings and quarterly scheduling; identifying instructional needs and providing onboarding guidance for new instructors; convening monthly instructor meetings to discuss courses and strengthen community; communicating with instructors about schedules, syllabi, course content, and logistical details; participating in program information sessions; supporting the career fair, mentoring program, and other initiatives that connect students with employers and industry partners; collaborating closely with academic advisors to ensure that program structures and student support practices align with student needs and promote successful progression through the program; and contributing to graduation and celebration activities for students completing the program. The Runstad Department of Real Estate within the College of Built Environments was established in 2017. The Department offers a Real Estate Major, a Real Estate Minor, a Master of Science in Real Estate, a Graduate Certificate in Real Estate, and a Graduate Certificate in Housing Studies. Together, these programs currently serve over 400 students. For more information about the Department, visit: https://re.be.uw.edu Qualifications Applicants for this teaching faculty position must meet the following minimum qualifications: 1) Master’s degree (or foreign equivalent) in real estate, urban planning, construction management, geography, economics, public policy, business related disciplines, or another field with clear overlap in real estate theory or practice, with degree completion required no later than September 2027. 2) Evidence, documented in the application materials, of a strong record of outstanding teaching or professional practice expertise in real estate or a closely related discipline. 3) Expertise in leading or coordinating academic programs. Instructions To ensure full consideration of your application, please submit all materials no later than October 15, 2026. Priority will be given to those applications received by that date. Applications will only be accepted via Interfolio: apply.interfolio.com/188858 . The position will remain open until filled. Applicants should articulate in their application documents how their past experiences and/or future plans in teaching, mentoring, and/or service promote a sense of belonging and foster a welcoming and inclusive environment. Applications should include the following materials: 1) A cover letter detailing your interest in the position, relevant qualifications, and experiences (maximum length of two single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 2) A curriculum vitae or resume (maximum length of five single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 3) A statement of teaching philosophy and evidence of teaching effectiveness (maximum length of two single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 4) A complete list of existing departmental courses, ranked in order of teaching preference, and any new courses you would like to propose to create and teach. For each course, include a brief description of your qualifications to teach it (maximum length of three single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 5) A statement demonstrating experience and success in leading and/or coordinating academic programs (maximum length of two single‑spaced pages, using 12‑point font) 6) Contact information for at least three referees who are prepared to provide letters of recommendation upon request (maximum length of one single‑spaced page, using 12‑point font) Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Equal Opportunity The University of Washington is committed to fostering an inclusive, respectful and welcoming community for all. As an equal opportunity employer, the University considers applicants for employment without regard to race, color, creed, religion, national origin, citizenship, sex, pregnancy, age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, disability, or veteran status consistent with UW Executive Order No. 81 . Benefits Information The University is committed to providing excellent benefits to our faculty. For a summary of available benefits, please see UWHR benefits information . Privacy Notice Your application materials may be used for employment consideration and shared in accordance with UW’s Privacy Policy. Disability Services The University of Washington is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education, and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact the Disability Services Office at 206-543-6450 or dso@uw.edu. Apply now The University of Washington is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. For help signing up, accessing your account, or submitting your application, please check out Interfolio's help and support section or get in touch via email at help@interfolio.com or phone at (877)997-8807. Title IX Notice The University of Washington does not discriminate on the basis of sex and prohibits sex discrimination and sex-based harassment in its education programs and activities, including in admission and employment. Refer to the UW policies page on the Title IX website to review (1) the University’s policies prohibiting sex- and gender-based violence, harassment, and discrimination and (2) the related procedures for addressing complaints. Inquiries about Title IX may be referred to the University’s Title IX Coordinator , the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights , or both. To report information or make a complaint about conduct that may constitute sex discrimination or sex-based harassment, submit an online Title IX report or email or call the Office of the Title IX Coordinator. Office of the Title IX Coordinator Valery Richardson, Title IX Coordinator 4311 11th Ave NE Seattle, WA 98105 206-221-7932 TitleIX@uw.edu ← Back to all positions University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
